# Docker ignore patterns are NOT recursive by default. `filepath.Match` semantics mean
# `__pycache__/` matches only a top-level `__pycache__` -- it never matched `app/core/__pycache__`
# or `tests/unit/__pycache__`. The image audit measured the result: 24 nested `__pycache__`
# directories inside /app and 73 `*.cpython-312-pytest-9.0.3.pyc` files, produced by a test run on
# the developer's host and carried into a production image by `COPY . .`
# (docs/security/audit/run-1/verification/containers/images/images.md, section 5b). Every pattern
# below is therefore anchored with `**/` unless it genuinely only exists at the context root.

# Virtualenv and byte-compiled output (the image builds its own venv at Dockerfile:9)
.venv/
**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[cod]

# Tool caches
**/.pytest_cache/
**/.mypy_cache/
**/.ruff_cache/
**/htmlcov/
**/.coverage
coverage.xml

# Secrets. `.env` sits at the context root, which is why the live vendor keys were verified
# absent from the image; the recursive form costs nothing and survives a context move.
.env
**/.env
**/.env.local
**/.env.*.local

# Local data (uploads, qdrant storage)
data/

# Not in scope today -- the build context is `backend/`, so the repo's `.git` was never reachable.
# The audit called that luck rather than policy: it breaks the moment a context moves up a level.
.git/
.gitignore

# Agent tooling config. No `backend/.claude/` exists today; the frontend context had one and it
# shipped inside the image, so the pattern is here pre-emptively rather than after the fact.
.claude/
